/**
 * @module compress
 *
 * Compression and decompression with several algorithms, both in memory
 * and as a stream, and reading and writing TAR and ZIP archives.
 *
 * ## Compression methods
 *
 * | Method    | Submodule            | Encode | Decode | Streaming | Notes |
 * |-----------|----------------------|--------|--------|-----------|-------|
 * | Deflate   | `compress.deflate`   | yes    | yes    | yes       | The raw compressed stream of RFC 1951. |
 * | Zlib      | `compress.zlib`      | yes    | yes    | yes       | The RFC 1950 wrapper around Deflate. It also re-exports Deflate's `GzipDecoder` and `GzipEncoder` as `DeflateDecoder` and `DeflateEncoder`. |
 * | Gzip      | `compress.gzip`      | yes    | yes    | yes       | The RFC 1952 wrapper around Deflate. `GzFile` lets you use a `.gz` file like an ordinary file. |
 * | Zstandard | `compress.zstd`      | yes    | yes    | yes       | Implemented in pure Rust (`zrip`). |
 * | LZ4       | `compress.lz4`       | yes    | yes    | yes       | The frame format, implemented with `lz4_flex`. |
 * | Bzip2     | `compress.bzip2`     | yes    | yes    | yes       | Implemented in pure Rust (`libbz2-rs-sys`). `BzFile` lets you use a `.bz2` file like an ordinary file. It only decodes the first stream of a bzip2 multistream, as the submodule's documentation explains. |
 * | Brotli    | `compress.brotli`    | yes    | yes    | decoding only | Implemented in pure Rust. The streaming encoder collects all its input and compresses it at once in `finish()`, instead of as it arrives. See the documentation of `BrotliEncoder`. |
 *
 * Every streaming submodule works the same way. There's a pair of
 * functions, `compress()` and `decompress()`, for when all the data is
 * already in memory, and a pair of classes, an encoder and a decoder,
 * for when the input arrives in pieces, or the output has to be used as
 * it's produced. The encoder has `write()`, `flush()`, `finish()` and
 * `reset()`, and the decoder has `read()`, `read_exact()`, `read_all()`
 * and `read_as_string()`. `total_in()`, `total_out()`, `available()`
 * and `finished()` are available on both, so you can inspect their
 * state.
 *
 * `compress.checksum` provides CRC-32 and Adler-32, which don't depend
 * on any compression method. `zip` uses them to check extracted
 * entries, but you can use them by themselves too.
 *
 * ## Archive formats
 *
 * | Format | Submodule        | Read | Write | Compression methods |
 * |--------|------------------|------|-------|----------------------|
 * | TAR    | `compress.tar`   | yes  | yes   | none, gzip and bzip2 (`COMPRESS_NONE`, `COMPRESS_GZIP` and `COMPRESS_BZIP`) |
 * | ZIP    | `compress.zip`   | yes  | yes   | stored, deflate and bzip2 (`ZIP_STORED`, `ZIP_DEFLATE` and `ZIP_BZIP2`), with zip64 for archives and files larger than the classic 4 GiB limit |
 *
 * Both `tar` and `zip` have a lower-level class, `tar.Tar` and
 * `zip.ZipArchive`, for building an archive one entry at a time or
 * inspecting one in detail, and a pair of functions, `compress()` and
 * `extract()`, for the common case of archiving a whole file or
 * directory, or extracting a whole archive. `zip.extract()` refuses to
 * write outside the destination directory, even if the archive contains
 * entry names with `../` in them, which is the "Zip Slip" attack. A ZIP
 * entry that can't be decoded, because it's unsupported or encrypted,
 * or because its CRC-32 doesn't match what the archive says, is
 * reported in that entry's `ZipItem.error`, instead of being silently
 * skipped or stopping the whole read.
 *
 * TAR doesn't currently support the LZMA (xz) compression that some
 * `.tar.xz` archives use, or Brotli, which has no established
 * convention for TAR. ZIP doesn't currently support LZMA (method 14),
 * PPMd (method 98), or either of the ZIP encryption schemes
 * (traditional PKWARE or AES). An encrypted entry is reported in
 * `ZipItem.error` instead of being attempted.
 *
 * @copyright 1995-2017 Jean-loup Gailly and Mark Adler
 * @copyright 2021, Richard Ore and Zuri contributors
 */

import @.deflate
import @.gzip
import @.checksum
import @.zstd
import @.lz4
import @.bzip2
import @.brotli
import @.tar
import @.zip
import @.zlib { * }
